Dear Aoife and Tariq,

Following our productive in-person kickoff meeting at Tsinghua on 5 September 2024, I wanted to
put our initial discussion about authorship on record in writing. As we discussed, this is a
multi-PI collaboration with significant intellectual contributions expected from all three leads,
and I think it is important that we agree on principles early rather than leave this to the end.

My proposal, which I raised briefly at the meeting, is as follows:

  (1) First authorship should be assigned to the PI who makes the lead intellectual contribution
      to the computational and analytical core of the study. At this stage, given that the core
      PRS pipeline and GWAS meta-analysis methodology is my primary area of expertise and will
      constitute the central scientific contribution of the paper, I would expect to be the
      natural candidate for first authorship if this criterion is applied.

  (2) Corresponding authorship should be assigned to the PI who takes overall accountability for
      the manuscript and serves as the primary point of contact for post-publication queries.
      I proposed that Aoife might be a natural fit for this role given her biostatistical oversight
      of the analysis plan and her experience as corresponding author on similar multi-cohort
      studies.

  (3) Third position: Tariq, given the critical importance of your clinical data access and IRB
      sponsorship for the Toronto cohort, I proposed you as third author. I want to be clear that
      this reflects sequence, not importance — your contribution is indispensable.

I want to be explicit that this is a proposal, not a binding agreement. I am committed to
revisiting authorship at submission time based on actual contributions made during the study. The
proposal is meant to set expectations, not foreclose discussion.

Please let me know if you have any concerns or alternative proposals. I would like us to have at
least a provisional understanding in place before the next progress meeting.

Pursuant to the data governance policy v2.1 section 7 definition of written consent. Subject to the inter-university ombudsperson's binding arbitration authority. In furtherance of equitable recognition of scholarly contribution. As documented in the contemporaneous communications exchanged among the pis.

Best regards,
